tructure of the 2-factor transfer digraph common for rectangular ...\n135\n\u0013\n\u0012 2k + 1\nk −s + 1\nk\nX\nk\nX\n\u0013\n=\n\u0013\n.\n| G(s)\n2k+1 |=\n\u0012k + 1\ni\n\u0013\u0012 k\ni + s\n\u0012k + 1\ni\n\u0013\u0012\nk\nk −i −s\n\u0013\n=\n\u00122k + 1\nk −s\ni=0\ni=0\nNow, from Lemma 19 we conclude that the components of the transfer di-\ngraph D∗\nm are ⟨S(s)\nm ⟩D∗m where 0 ≤s ≤⌊m/2⌋. Note that the digraph ⟨S(0)\nm ⟩D∗m is\nthe component which contains the vertex 1m. It implies that ⟨S(0)\nm ⟩D∗m = A∗\nm. Hav-\n2 ⌋\n[\n⌊m/2⌋\n[\ning in mind that D∗\nm = A∗\nm∪(\n⌊m\ns=1\nB∗(s)\nm ) =\ns=0\n⟨S(s)\nm ⟩D∗m and | S(s1)\nm\n|>| S(s2)\nm\n| for\n1 ≤s1 < s2 ≤⌊m/2⌋, we conclude that B∗(s)\nm\n= ⟨S(s)\nm ⟩D∗m for all s = 1, 2, . . . , ⌊m/2⌋.\nConsequently, all the components B∗(s)\nm\n(1 ≤s ≤⌊m/2⌋) are bipartite digraphs.\nLemma 20 and Lemma 21 further complete the proof of the Theorem 5.\nAcknowledgements. The authors are indebted to the anonymous referees\nfor their valuable suggestions and helpful comments which improved the clarity\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Structure of the 2-factor Transfer Digraph common for Thin Cylinder, Torus and Klein Bottle Grid Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.13779)\nciteturn11academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Title: The Structure of the 2-factor Transfer Digraph common for Thin Cylinder, Torus and Klein Bottle Grid Graphs\nTitle: The Structure of the 2-factor Transfer Digraph common for Thin Cylinder, Torus and Klein Bottle Grid Graphs\nAuthors: Jelena Đokić, Ksenija Doroslovački, Olga Bodroža-Pantić\nDate: Wed Dec 28 10:48:12 2022\n\nWe prove that the transfer digraph ${\\cal D}^*_{C,m}$ needed for the enumeration of 2-factors in the thin cylinder $TnC_{m}(n)$, torus $TG_{m}(n)$ and Klein bottle $KB_m(n)$ (all grid graphs of the fixed width $m$ and with $m \\cdot n$ vertices), when $m$ is odd, has only two components of order $2^{m-1}$ which are isomorphic. When $m$ is even, ${\\cal D}^*_{C,m}$ has $ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{m}{2} \\right\\rfloor + 1$ components which orders can be expressed via binomial coefficients and all but one of the components are bipartite digraphs. The proof is based on the application of recently obtained results concerning the related transfer digraph for linear grid graphs (rectangular, thick cylinder and Moebius strip).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Structure of the 2-factor Transfer Digraph Common for Rectangular, Thick Cylinder and Moebius Strip Grid Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.00317)\nciteturn11academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Title: The Structure of the 2-factor Transfer Digraph Common for Rectangular, Thick Cylinder and Moebius Strip Grid Graphs ... In addition, we prove that the set of vertices of each component consists of all the binary $m$-words for which the difference of numbers of zeros in odd and even positions is constant.\nTitle: The Structure of the 2-factor Transfer Digraph Common for Rectangular, Thick Cylinder and Moebius Strip Grid Graphs\nAuthors: Jelena Đokić, Ksenija Doroslovački, Olga Bodroža-Pantić\nDate: Thu Dec  1 07:09:33 2022\n\nIn this paper, we prove that all but one of the components of the transfer digraph ${\\cal D}^*_m$ needed for the enumeration of 2-factors in the rectangular, thick cylinder and Moebius strip grid graphs of the fixed width $m$ $(m \\in N)$ are bipartite digraphs and that their orders could be expressed in term of binomial coefficients. In addition, we prove that the set of vertices of each component consists of all the binary $m$-words for which the difference of numbers of zeros in odd and even positions is constant.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA short note on the order of the double reduced 2-factor transfer digraph for rectangular grid graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.04155)\nciteturn11academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Title: A short note on the order of the double reduced 2-factor transfer digraph for rectangular grid graphs\nTitle: A short note on the order of the double reduced 2-factor transfer digraph for rectangular grid graphs\nAuthors: Jelena Đokic\nDate: Tue Aug  8 09:36:50 2023\n\nWe prove that the order of the double reduced 2-factor transfer digraph ${\\cal R}^{**}_{m}$ which is needed for the enumeration of the spanning unions of cycles in the rectangular grid graph $P_m \\times P_n$ ($m,n \\in N$), when $m$ is odd, is equal to $\\displaystyle \\mid V({\\cal R}^{**}_{m}) \\mid = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[{m+1 \\choose (m-1)/2 } + {(m+1)/2 \\choose \\lfloor (m+1)/4 \\rfloor}\\right].$--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) The Structure of the 2-factor Transfer Digraph common for Thin Cylinder, Torus and Klein Bottle Grid Graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/366656851_The_Structure_of_the_2-factor_Transfer_Digraph_common_for_Thin_Cylinder_Torus_and_Klein_Bottle_Grid_Graphs)\nciteturn11search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; In this way, we reduced the transfer digraph ... such a 2-factor exists at all, then there exist at most two possible choices of vertical edges for ... component, so such gluing does not reduce the number of components.\n\nc b a f e d and a b c d e f\n\nd b f a e c , respectively.\n\nThe digraph\n\nD ud\n\nis responsible for building alpha words, and the other one is respon-\n\nsible for the adjacency of columns in the code matrices in the following sense:\n\nLemma 1\n\n(Characterization of 2-factors for circular grid graphs)\n\n.\n\nThe code matrix\n\n[α i,j]m×n\n\nfor\n\nany 2-factor of\n\nG∈ {TnC m(n)\n\n,\n\nTG(p)\n\nm(n)\n\n,\n\nKB(p)\n\nm(n)}\n\nsatisﬁes the following properties (\n\nα m+1,j\n\ndef\n\n=\n\nα 1,j):\n\n1. Column condition: For every ﬁxed j (1≤j≤n),\n\nthe ordered pairs (α i,j,α i+1,j), where 1≤i≤m, must be arcs i